  "learn.b.password": "What Makes a Strong Password?\nAuthentication protects your digital identity. Attackers do not guess passwords manually; they use supercomputers and graphics cards capable of testing billions of password combinations per second.\n\nKey Concepts & Technical Terms:\n- Password Entropy: The measure of randomness and unpredictability in a password, calculated in bits. Each additional character adds exponential difficulty for attackers.\n- Credential Stuffing: Automated attacks using massive lists of stolen username/password pairs from previous website breaches to test against hundreds of other online services.\n- Salted Cryptographic Hashing: A security standard where unique random strings (salts) are added to passwords before hashing, rendering precomputed cracking dictionaries (Rainbow Tables) useless.\n- TOTP (Time-based One-Time Password): A temporary 6-digit verification code generated on your smartphone authenticator app every 30 seconds, immune to mobile network eavesdropping.\n\nWhy Complexity Rules Failed:\nRules forcing symbols and numbers often lead users to create predictable formulas like 'Password123!'. A 4-word passphrase made of common words is far easier to remember and orders of magnitude harder for computers to crack.",
  "learn.b.phishing": "What is Phishing?\nPhishing is a social engineering attack where threat actors impersonate trusted organizations—such as commercial banks, telecom providers, government agencies, or universities—to trick individuals into revealing credentials, PINs, or financial secrets.\n\nKey Concepts & Technical Terms:\n- Credential Harvester: A fraudulent webpage designed to mimic legitimate login screens (e.g. online banking or webmail) that instantly logs passwords and one-time codes entered by victims.\n- Homograph / Look-Alike Domains: Deceptive domain registrations replacing characters with visually identical counterparts (such as replacing the letter 'o' with the number '0', or adding misleading suffixes like '-verify.et').\n- Heuristic Threat Detection: Rule-based analysis used by scanners (like ABBAY CYBERGUARD M1) to identify suspicious patterns, obfuscated redirects, and fraudulent layouts without needing a previously known blacklist entry.\n\nWhy Threat Actors Target Humans:\nModern operating systems and encryption are mathematically strong. Attackers therefore bypass technical firewalls by targeting human cognitive shortcuts: creating manufactured urgency, invoking authority, or promising sudden financial gains.",
  "learn.b.privacy": "What is Digital Privacy?\nPrivacy is not about having something to hide; it is about retaining control over who has access to your personal identity, daily routines, financial status, and relationships.\n\nKey Concepts & Technical Terms:\n- Personally Identifiable Information (PII): Any data that can identify an individual directly or indirectly—including full legal names, National ID (Fayda) numbers, Kebele ID cards, phone numbers, and facial photos.\n- Digital Footprint: The permanent trail of social posts, uploaded documents, tagged photos, and location check-ins left behind when using online services.\n- Pretexting: An adversarial technique where an attacker invents a believable scenario (pretext) to manipulate a victim into releasing information or transferring funds.\n- OSINT (Open Source Intelligence): Methods used by attackers to harvest publicly visible data from social media, public databases, and search engines to construct targeted attacks.\n\nWhy Oversharing is Dangerous:\nAttackers rarely hack accounts with high-tech code when they can simply gather your personal history from public posts to answer your security questions or impersonate your relatives.",
  "learn.b.response": "What is Incident Response?\nIncident response is an organized approach to addressing and managing the aftermath of a security breach or cyberattack. The goal is to handle the situation in a way that limits damage, reduces recovery time, and preserves evidence for law enforcement.\n\nKey Concepts & Technical Terms:\n- Containment: Immediate tactical actions taken to stop an active attack from spreading across accounts or networks without destroying vital evidence.\n- Chain of Custody: The chronological documentation demonstrating who collected, handled, and analyzed digital evidence, ensuring it remains valid in legal proceedings.\n- Volatile Evidence: Fragile system data—such as open network connections, running memory (RAM), and temporary cache—that disappears once a device is powered down.\n- Indicators of Compromise (IoCs): Forensic artifacts—such as fraudulent phone numbers, malicious URLs, file hashes, and IP addresses—that prove an attack occurred.\n\nWhy Calm Discipline Matters:\nPanicking during an attack often leads victims to delete messages, wipe phones, or confront scammers directly. This destroys the evidence needed to recover funds and identify perpetrators.",
  "learn.b.website": "What is Defensive Website Security?\nWeb security encompasses the defensive controls, architectural configurations, and cryptographic protections implemented to protect websites and web applications from eavesdropping, tampering, and malicious exploitation.\n\nKey Concepts & Technical Terms:\n- TLS/HTTPS: Cryptographic protocols that encrypt communication between web browsers and servers, ensuring confidentiality, data integrity, and server authentication.\n- HTTP Strict Transport Security (HSTS): A defensive header instructing browsers to strictly communicate over HTTPS, preventing SSL-stripping and downgrade attacks.\n- Content Security Policy (CSP): A powerful header specifying approved sources for scripts, styles, and images, neutralizing Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) attacks.\n- SameSite & HttpOnly Cookies: Secure cookie flags that block JavaScript access to session tokens and prevent Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF).\n\nPadlock Icon vs. Authentic Trust:\nA valid HTTPS padlock means the connection is encrypted—it does NOT prove the website owner is honest. Attackers can easily obtain free SSL certificates for deceptive phishing domains.",
