  "learn.warn.password": "Warning Signs & Password Traps:\n\n- Predictable Personal Information: Passwords containing your birth year, child's name, favorite Ethiopian Premier League club, or car license plate.\n- Reusing the Same Root Password: Using 'Selam2024' on one site and 'Selam2024!' on another.\n- Unsolicited One-Time Codes: Receiving an SMS with a verification code you did not request means someone has already entered your correct password and is blocked only by 2FA.\n- Browser Compromise Alerts: Red or yellow browser alerts stating that your saved password was found in a public data breach.",
  "learn.warn.phishing": "Critical Red Flags & Deception Traps:\n\n- Manufactured Urgency: Extreme time pressure ('10 minutes remaining', 'Account will be deactivated today') is the single most common deception marker.\n- Sender Inconsistency: Ethiopian banks and Ethio Telecom use registered alphanumeric sender headers (e.g. 'CBE', 'telebirr', 'EthioTel'), never standard 10-digit mobile numbers (+2519...).\n- Domain Subdomain Tricks: Look at the domain structure. In 'telebirr.com.et.verify-portal.net', the actual domain is 'verify-portal.net', NOT telebirr.\n- Requests for Secret PINs or OTPs: Legitimate organizations will NEVER ask you for your mobile money PIN or SMS verification codes via phone call, chat, or email.\n- Shortened & Obfuscated Links: Unexplained bit.ly, tinyurl, or raw IP addresses masking the true destination.",
  "learn.warn.privacy": "Critical Red Flags & Privacy Traps:\n\n- Requests for Urgent Financial Help from New Numbers: A friend or relative messaging from an unfamiliar number claiming an emergency and asking for direct mobile transfers.\n- Viral Quizzes Inquiring About Life History: Quizzes or games asking for childhood street names, first car model, or mother's village.\n- Unnecessary App Permissions: A flashlight, calculator, or offline game demanding access to contacts, call logs, SMS, and precise location.\n- Unredacted Document Uploads: Sharing photos of receipts, bank transfer slips, boarding passes, or identification cards online.",
  "learn.warn.response": "Warning Signs of Active Compromise:\n\n- Sudden Cellular Disconnection: Persistent 'No Service' or 'Emergency Calls Only' in areas with known good reception—a primary indicator of SIM-swap fraud.\n- Unrecognized Login Alerts: Notifications from Google, Telegram, or banking apps showing logins from unknown devices, browsers, or foreign IP addresses.\n- Unexplained Balance Changes: Micro-debits, unexpected SMS transaction confirmations, or altered account balances.\n- Password Reset Failures: Being logged out of your email or messaging apps and finding that your password has been changed without your knowledge.",
  "learn.warn.website": "Critical Red Flags & Architectural Vulnerabilities:\n\n- Missing HTTPS / Broken Padlock: Browsers displaying 'Not Secure' or a red strike-through padlock icon.\n- Mixed Content Warnings: Secure HTTPS pages loading scripts, images, or stylesheets over unencrypted HTTP.\n- Verbose Server Fingerprints: HTTP response headers revealing detailed underlying OS, PHP, or Apache/Nginx build versions.\n- Permissive CORS (Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *): Allowing arbitrary external websites to read sensitive API responses on behalf of authenticated users.\n- Missing Cookie Protection: Session cookies missing 'Secure', 'HttpOnly', or 'SameSite=Lax/Strict' attributes.",
  "learn.your_ans": "Your answer",
  "m1.act.IND-01": "Never enter login credentials, payment details, or personal data on a bare numeric IP address.",
  "m1.act.IND-02": "Disregard any company name shown before the @ character and inspect the host that follows it.",
  "m1.act.IND-03": "Double-check character spelling closely or type the official organization web address manually.",
  "m1.act.IND-04": "Identify the two rightmost segments (e.g. example.com) to find who actually controls the domain.",
  "m1.act.IND-05": "Do not submit sensitive personal details or passwords over plain unencrypted HTTP.",
  "m1.act.IND-06": "Ask the sender for the complete destination link or verify their request through another channel.",
  "m1.act.IND-07": "Never use links in urgent messages to log in. Open the provider’s official app or site directly.",
  "m1.act.IND-08": "Inspect the primary domain name specifically rather than relying on a quick glance at the link.",
  "m1.act.IND-09": "Treat heavily encoded, unreadable URLs with suspicion, especially from unfamiliar contacts.",
  "m1.act.IND-10": "Confirm why an uncommon port is specified before communicating with the service.",
  "m1.act.TXT-01": "Never share OTP codes, PINs, or passwords with anyone, even if they claim to be official customer service.",
  "m1.act.TXT-02": "Pause and calm down. Independently contact the organization using their known, official phone number before taking action.",
  "m1.act.TXT-03": "Do not click links inside the message. Open the official mobile app or visit the verified web address directly.",
  "m1.act.TXT-04": "Ignore unsolicited prize claims. You cannot win a lottery or raffle you never entered.",
  "m1.act.TXT-05": "Never pay an advance fee to receive a prize, job, or loan. Genuine organizations never operate this way.",
  "m1.act.TXT-06": "Do not tap links embedded in unsolicited messages. Inspect the extracted link details below.",
  "m1.act.TXT-07": "Never download or install APK files received via chat. Install apps only from the official Google Play Store.",
  "m1.act.TXT-08": "Check the sender full email address. Treat any official business claim from a public webmail domain as fraudulent.",
  "m1.act.TXT-09": "Never send bank account numbers or financial details to unknown contacts. Legitimate payouts or organizations do not require account collection through unverified messaging.",
  "m1.act.TXT-10": "Do not provide personal profile data, occupation, or demographic details in response to informal questionnaires, messages, or unverified prize offers.",
